# ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ LESSON 2 · TAU: the circle inside every sound        │
# └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
#
# Open any aither patch and you'll see TAU everywhere:
# sin(TAU * phasor(f)), k = (TAU × pitch)², pan angles.
# It's the most important number in the language, so it
# gets a lesson of its own.
#
# ▶ WHAT TAU IS
#
#   TAU = 6.2832... = one full turn of a circle, measured
#   in "radians" (the natural unit for angles in maths).
#
#     a quarter turn  = TAU / 4
#     half a turn     = TAU / 2
#     a full turn     = TAU
#
#   You might know π (pi, 3.1416). π is HALF a turn, which
#   is why old formulas are full of 2π. TAU is just 2π with
#   its own name, because a full turn is what you almost
#   always mean.
#
# ▶ A SINE WAVE IS A CIRCLE SEEN FROM THE SIDE
#
#   Imagine a dot riding round a circle. Watch only its
#   height: it rises, falls, rises... That height, drawn
#   over time, IS a sine wave. sin(angle) gives the height
#   of the dot at that angle.
#
#   So a 220 Hz tone is a dot going round a circle 220
#   times every second.
#
# ▶ PHASOR: HOW FAR ROUND THE CIRCLE
#
#   phasor(220) counts 0 → 1 over and over, 220 times a
#   second. It's "what fraction of the lap are we on":
#     0.25 = a quarter of the way round, 0.5 = halfway.
#
#   TAU * phasor(220) turns that fraction into an angle,
#   and sin(...) turns the angle into the height:
#
#     sin(TAU * phasor(220))      ← this IS osc(sin, 220)
#
#   Every oscillator in aither is these three steps:
#   count laps (phasor), make an angle (× TAU), read the
#   shape (sin, saw, tri, sqr...).
#
# ▶ PHASE: WHERE ON THE CIRCLE YOU START
#
#   Two dots on the same circle, going the same speed,
#   one half a turn ahead: when one is at the top, the
#   other is at the bottom. Add their heights and you get
#   zero, forever. Sounds can CANCEL. Noise-cancelling
#   headphones work exactly like that. (That's the puzzle.)
#
# ▶ THIS PATCH: one part per idea, three seconds each
#
#   1. osc(sin, 220), written out the long way
#   2. the same sine, plus a copy a quarter turn ahead:
#      same note, about 1.4 times louder: the two
#      heights partly add up
#   3. a copy HALF a turn ahead: ...silence. Not a bug!
#   4. a circle in stereo: cos in the left ear and sin in
#      the right. The two ears hear the dot's left-right
#      and up-down, so the sound seems to spin.

let pos = sweep
let lap = phasor(220)

play longway:
  sin(TAU * lap) * 0.2

play quarter:
  (sin(TAU * lap) + sin(TAU * lap + TAU / 4)) * 0.2

play half:
  (sin(TAU * lap) + sin(TAU * lap + TAU / 2)) * 0.2

play spin:
  let angle = TAU * phasor(0.5)
  let tone  = sin(TAU * lap) * 0.2
  [tone * (0.5 + 0.5 * cos(angle)), tone * (0.5 + 0.5 * sin(angle))]

let which = int(pos / 3) mod 4
let w1 = if which == 0 then 1 else 0
let w2 = if which == 1 then 1 else 0
let w3 = if which == 2 then 1 else 0
let w4 = if which == 3 then 1 else 0

longway * w1 + quarter * w2 + half * w3 + spin * w4

# ▶ TRY THIS
#
#   1. In "half", change TAU / 2 to TAU / 2.1. Almost
#      opposite, so almost silent: a ghost of the note.
#   2. Two notes, one circle. sin(TAU * lap * 2) goes round
#      twice per lap: an octave up, locked perfectly in step
#      with the original.
#   3. Spin faster: phasor(0.5) → phasor(4). At 4 laps a
#      second the motion turns into a wobble.
#   4. Physics uses TAU too. A spring that rings at f Hz
#      has stiffness (TAU × f)²: TAU × f is "angles per
#      second", the speed of the dot. Lesson 11 builds one.
#   5. Draw it in numbers. Audit "half" alone (delete the
#      other three from the last line):
#        ./aither audit edc/02_tau.aither 1
#      RMS is about -inf: true silence, from two sounds.
#
# ▶ PUZZLE → edc/02_tau_puzzle.aither
